  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 40,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppppppbp/5np1/8/2PP4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [
    "Bg7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The fianchetto completed, the bishop to g7 — a King's Indian."
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 45.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppppppbp/5np1/8/2PPP3/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "e4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White builds the full centre with e4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 48,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "d6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 52.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "f3"
   ],
   "spoken": "And here is the recommendation against the King's Indian — the Sämisch, marked by f3."
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 59,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR w KQ - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "The idea of f3, familiar from the Sicilian: cement the e4-pawn."
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 82.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR b KQ - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Be3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Not the premature d5 — instead develop the bishop to e3; the h4 storm is the ultimate idea."
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 88,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/2p5/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR w KQ - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The Sämisch is the one King's Indian line where White routinely castles long."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 92.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/2p5/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP2N1PP/R2QKB1R b KQ - 1 7",
   "line": [
    "Nge2"
   ],
   "spoken": "Two reasons for f3: it cements the centre, and it stops Black harassing the bishop with the knight to g4 — and White won't give up that bishop except for its counterpart."
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 111,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PpP3/2N1BP2/PP2N1PP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "cxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 112.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKB1R b KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "Nxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 114,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Black has reached an Accelerated-Dragon-type structure — but this is not the best way to meet the Sämisch; White is just solidly better now."
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 132,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/R3KB1R b KQ - 2 9",
   "line": [
    "Qd2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen to d2 is good. White could also retreat the knight to c2 to dodge the trade, but the trade is fine here."
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 154,
   "fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ - 3 10",
   "line": [
    "Re8"
   ],
   "spoken": "A lesson in flexibility: begin the game with one plan, but be ready to stop on a dime. White planned a quick attack, but with a positional edge in hand, the plan can change."
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 172.5,
   "fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/2KR1B1R b - - 4 10",
   "line": [
    "O-O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "The rook to e8 was a bit of an empty move — so White castles long, intentions perfectly clear."
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 183.5,
   "fen": "r2qr1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/2KR1B1R w - - 5 11",
   "line": [
    "Bd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "White goes straight for the throat, conventional style — h4, h5, open the h-file, checkmate."
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 188,
   "fen": "r2qr1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/2KR1B1R b - - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "h4"
   ],
   "spoken": "So — h4, no delay. And the pawn will be sacrificed; that is the whole point of the attack."
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 193,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/2KR1B1R w - - 1 12",
   "line": [
    "Qa5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 218.5,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R b - - 2 12",
   "line": [
    "Kb1"
   ],
   "spoken": "And the Russian-school move: the king to b1. Once you have castled long, this is a healthy prophylactic step — the king is simply safer, off the c-file where Black's rooks will point."
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 224.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R w - - 3 13",
   "line": [
    "Rac8"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 232.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q6P/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R b - - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "h5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now the attack flows more smoothly with the king tucked away — not globally safe, but harder to reach, buying time to orchestrate the kingside storm."
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 241.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/q6n/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R w - - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "Nxh5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Careful now — g4 to chase the knight runs into a nasty reply."
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 280,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/q6n/2P1P3/1NN1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R b - - 1 14",
   "line": [
    "Nb3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight to g3 would hit the rook. So, in the spirit of prepare-slowly-attack-fast, White drops the knight back to b3 — reducing the tension on d4, forcing the queen to move, and untying White's hands for the attack."
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 309,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/q6n/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R w - - 0 14",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 315,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/q6n/2P1P3/1NN1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R b - - 1 14",
   "line": [
    "Nb3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The point of easing the d4 tension is to attack single-mindedly on the kingside, with no more worry about trades. If Black takes on c3, White recaptures and has removed Black's best piece — structure be damned, well worth it.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 373,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/4q2n/2P1P3/1NN1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R w - - 2 15",
   "line": [
    "Qe5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The battle is simple: who mates first — White on the kingside, Black on the queenside. And the alarm bells ring: a move that failed before can work now. Consider g4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 29,
   "t": 403,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/4q2n/2P1P1P1/1NN1BP2/PP1Q4/1K1R1B1R b - - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "g4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Because the queen has repositioned to the centre, where it is most exposed, after g4 and the knight to g3 a new resource appears."
  },
  {
   "ply": 31,
   "t": 409.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/4q3/2P1PBP1/1NN2Pn1/PP1Q4/1K1R1B1R b - - 2 16",
   "line": [
    "Ng3",
    "Bf4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop to f4 forks the queen and the knight — a fake fork, since Black can take on f1 and hit the queen."
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 411,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/4q3/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP1Q4/1K1R1n1R w - - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Nxf1"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 33,
   "t": 413,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np2p1/4q3/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP1Q4/1K3R1R b - - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Rdxf1"
   ],
   "spoken": "But remember the goal — not to win material, just to remove that knight and finally free the queen for the h-file and mate on h2."
  },
  {
   "ply": 34,
   "t": 426,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2npq1p1/8/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP1Q4/1K3R1R w - - 1 18",
   "line": [
    "Qe6"
   ],
   "spoken": "White is far closer to mating than Black is to anything on the queenside — and that whole wing is intact, thanks to the king move."
  },
  {
   "ply": 35,
   "t": 435,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2npq1p1/8/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP5Q/1K3R1R b - - 2 18",
   "line": [
    "Qh2"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here comes the queen to h2 — big trouble, because taking on h7 leads to mate in just a couple of moves."
  },
  {
   "ply": 36,
   "t": 463.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/3pq1p1/4n3/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP5Q/1K3R1R w - - 3 19",
   "line": [
    "Ne5"
   ],
   "spoken": "With so many pieces aimed at the king, Black is busted — his own queen stuck out of play, unable to help."
  },
  {
   "ply": 40,
   "t": 466,
   "fen": "2r1rb2/pp1bppkp/3pq1p1/4n3/2P1PBP1/1NN2P2/PP6/1K3R1R w - - 0 21",
   "line": [
    "Qh6",
    "Bf8",
    "Qg7+",
    "Kxg7"
   ],
   "spoken": "A very typical idea: the queen to h8 would be mate but for the bishop — so eliminate it with the bishop to h6, and it is mate anyway, thanks to the pin.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 498,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "White never even got touched — the pawn structure stayed whole. The Sämisch, with f3, was first played by Sämisch himself in 1925, one of those openings named for the man who first played it.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 557,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR w KQ - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 573.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 577,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "f3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The Sämisch has two ideas. First, protect the e4-pawn — because against the King's Indian it is not how much centre you control but how well: the bishop guards the d-pawn, a pawn guards the e-pawn.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 583,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR b KQ - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O",
    "Be3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 593,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/2p5/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR w KQ - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Then castle long and roll h4-h5. As it happens, c5 is one of Black's best replies — a pawn sacrifice; taking it is possible but leads to wild complications where Black is fine.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 598.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/5np1/2p5/2P1P3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR w KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "dxc5",
